 **Wiring**
-- Orthogonal only, every coordinate on the 10-unit grid, no diagonals.
+- Orthogonal, every coordinate on the 10-unit grid.
+- **The one exception is a cross-coupled pair** (latch, regenerative comparator, level shifter):
+  its two gate-to-opposite-drain wires are drawn as diagonals forming an X between the two
+  mirrored devices. Put the gate stubs on the inner sides and end each diagonal on a stub end
+  and on the opposite drain track. The diagonals cross in their middles with no dot there; a
+  crossing is not a connection, and a netlist round trip confirms it. Use diagonals nowhere else.
 - Crossings without a dot are not connections; xschem draws the dots at T-junctions.
-- Bulks are wired to their source or rail, never left to a label alone. Even inside a branch, the
-  PMOS bulk goes to the supply and the NMOS bulk to ground, unless the source netlist ties it
-  elsewhere.
+- Every net is a wire, including long ones across the sheet. A `lab_pin` names a node (an
+  internal net such as `a`, `b`, `inb`) and never replaces a wire.
+- **Bulk.** When the bulk is tied to its supply by default, which is the usual case, wire it to
+  the rail, not to its own source: a short jog sideways next to the source wire, landing on the
+  rail. NMOS and PMOS follow the same rule (PMOS bulk to the positive rail of its own row, NMOS
+  bulk to ground) with the same jog. Deviate when the source netlist ties the bulk elsewhere or
+  when the schematic is meant to show a different bulk connection (body bias, bulk-driven, source
+  tied against the body effect): then draw that connection deliberately so the difference is
+  visible. Never leave a bulk to a label alone.
 
 **What to avoid** (each of these was seen)
-- Net-label soup: one label per terminal, with names colliding ("vbpvbp", "vddvss").
+- Net-label soup: a `lab_pin` stub on every terminal or between columns instead of wires, with
+  names colliding ("vbpvbp", "vddvss").
 - Bulk or gate stubs from neighbouring devices that touch. xschem joins touching wire ends and
   labels, which merges nets **silently**.
 - Leftover labels under a hand-drawn wire. They are harmless only while they agree with the wire;
   once someone edits the wire, they keep the old connection alive.
 
+## Symbols for the cell itself
+
+- Look for a ready-made symbol first: the PDK's standard cells (for IHP,
+  `libs.tech/xschem/sg13cmos5l_stdcells/`), then the user's other designs. If nothing fits 1:1,
+  adapt the closest standard-cell symbol (triangle and bubble from `inv_1` / `buf_1` for an
+  inverter- or buffer-like cell) instead of drawing a box.
+- PDK standard-cell symbols (`type=primitive`) carry VDD/VSS as properties. A cell with several
+  or non-standard supplies needs real pins: `type=subcircuit`, `format="@name @pinlist @symname"`,
+  `template="name=x1"`, positive supplies on top, ground at the bottom, signals at (±40, 0), all
+  pins on the 10-unit grid.
+- Pin order = `.subckt` port order (the order of the `B 5` boxes). Keep the credit line of the
+  source symbol in the header (the IHP symbols are Apache-2.0).
+
+## Testbenches around the cell
+
+Seen in Christoph Maier's hand-drawn level-shifter testbenches (2026-10-04). The wire rules above
+apply unchanged.
+
+- Left to right: sources, device under test (DUT), loads. The DUT sits close to the sources, a few
+  hundred units away.
+- Sources are vertical, + on top, minus down to the ground bus. From left to right: the highest
+  supply, the lower supplies, then the input signal.
+- Supply and input nets are wires run above the DUT in nested L-shaped paths: the leftmost source
+  (highest supply) gets the topmost wire, the next source the next one down, the input the lowest,
+  so they do not cross on the way to the DUT. At the DUT the lower supply goes to the left supply
+  pin of the symbol, so the nesting carries into the pins. Accept a crossing only where it is
+  unavoidable (a second DUT further right that needs the lower supply).
+- One ground bus along the bottom, with a single `gnd` symbol at its left end. Source minus pins,
+  DUT ground pins and load capacitors are wired to it; no `gnd` symbol per element.
+- A load hangs below the output wire: a wire from the DUT output down to the top of the capacitor,
+  the bottom of the capacitor on the ground bus. The node between two DUTs is a straight wire with
+  its load hanging from a junction.
+- Name each net with a `lab_pin` sitting on its wire, at the source and at the DUT end (`vddh`,
+  `vddl`, `in`, `out`), not on a stub.
+- Title at the top left, the analysis code block (`code_shown`) under the circuit at the left.
